---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import reader from '../../assets/projects/glyph.png';
---

<Layout
  title="Glyph"
  description="An editor and reader for Markdown and mathematical notes, built with Next.js, Clerk and Convex."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Glyph"
      description="Edit and publish Markdown & mathematical notes."
      stack="Next.js / TypeScript / Clerk / Convex"
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="https://glyph-gules.vercel.app/p/test">
        Example reader ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/glyph">
        View source ↗
      </a>
    </CaseHeader>
    <figure class="case-image">
      <a
        class="case-image-link"
        href={reader.src}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        aria-label="Open Glyph screenshot at full size (new tab)"
      >
        <Image
          src={reader}
          alt="Glyph’s public reader showing a mechanics note with mathematical equations"
          widths={[480, 800, 1440]}
          sizes="90vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={95}
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        The public example reader, captured from the application. Select the
        image to enlarge.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Private work, public notes</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I wanted a way to prepare Markdown and mathematical notes privately,
          then share a readable version when it was ready. Glyph keeps editing
          and publishing connected without making every saved draft public.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project has involved reviewing the editing workflow, configuring
          authentication and testing who can retrieve a note, as well as working
          on the implementation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div class="glyph-feature">
      <div>
        <span class="glyph-word">Glyph</span>
        <p>
          Markdown, maths
          <br />
          and a link to share.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="publish-flow" aria-label="Publishing workflow">
        <span>Write a private draft</span>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
        <span>Edit & preview</span>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
        <span>Publish at your own URL</span>
        <p>Unpublish to withdraw public access.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Editing & preview</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Create a note in the BlockNote editor or import a Markdown or LaTeX
          file. Notes support headings, lists, code and mathematical notation,
          with a preview of the rendered document before publishing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reader includes heading navigation, code copying and print styles.
          LaTeX imports convert supported text and equations into Markdown;
          unsupported commands remain as source with a conversion notice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Implementation</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Next.js renders notes using remark, rehype and KaTeX. Clerk handles
          sign-in, and Convex stores note metadata and source files. Private
          drafts are retrieved for the signed-in owner.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="source-example"
          tabindex="0"
          aria-label="Scrollable code example"
        ><code>const ownerId = await requireOwner(ctx);
const noteId = ctx.db.normalizeId('notes', id);
const note = noteId ? await ctx.db.get('notes', noteId) : null;
return note?.ownerId === ownerId ? note : null;</code></pre>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/glyph/blob/main/convex/notes.ts"
        >
          Read the ownership check ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Handling content safely</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          A sign-in screen alone does not protect a draft. Convex checks the
          verified user against the note’s owner when reading or changing
          private content. Public metadata and source reads separately require
          the note to be published.
        </p>
        <p>
          Uploads are checked for supported text formats, valid UTF-8 and size
          before storage. Rendering discards raw HTML and sanitises the Markdown
          tree before KaTeX adds maths markup, with trusted commands disabled
          and expansion limits applied.
        </p>
        <p>
          LaTeX conversion is deliberately limited: it converts supported
          content without executing a TeX engine, loading packages or fetching
          external files. Preserving unsupported source with a notice is more
          useful than silently losing it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Publishing</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Choose a custom URL when publishing a note. Published notes must be
          unpublished before editing, so saving a draft does not change the live
          document. Unpublishing also removes public access to the note.
        </p>
        <p>
          The source is read through access-checked actions rather than a
          permanent public storage URL. Unpublishing blocks subsequent public
          requests, although it cannot recall content someone has already
          downloaded.
        </p>
        <p>
          The test suite covers note access, file uploads, rendering and
          publication, including unpublishing and deleting notes.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/glyph/blob/main/tests/notes-security.test.ts"
        >
          View tests ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Challenges & learning</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          The main design problem is keeping publication separate from ordinary
          saving, including when another tab has an older copy open. Draft saves
          check the note’s revision and reject conflicting updates; published
          notes must be unpublished before editing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Working on those boundaries has helped me distinguish authentication,
          ownership and public access. Tests for another user’s note,
          unpublished content and malicious markup make those rules more
          concrete than simply describing a page as private.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <a class="next-project" href="/projects/portfolio">
      <span>Next project</span>
      <strong>
        Personal Portfolio <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </strong>
    </a>
  </article>
</Layout>
